\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 3. 
\f3\fs39 args_schema
\f0\fs36  \'97 Pydantic validation on tool inputs\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs26 \cf0 from pydantic import BaseModel, Field\
from typing import Literal\
\
class SeatBookingInput(BaseModel):\
    movie_title: str = Field(description="Exact movie title")\
    seat_count: int = Field(ge=1, le=10, description="Number of seats")\
    preferred_row: Literal["front", "middle", "back"] = Field(default="middle")\
\
@tool("book_seats", args_schema=SeatBookingInput)\
def book_seats(movie_title: str, seat_count: int, preferred_row: str) -> str:\
    """Book seats for a movie."""\
    ...\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Inspect what the model actually sees:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 print(book_seats.args)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Without the 
\f2\fs26 @tool
\f1\fs24  decorator, a plain function has no 
\f2\fs26 .args
\f1\fs24  at all \'97 
\f2\fs26 AttributeError
\f1\fs24 . With the decorator you get names and types. With 
\f2\fs26 args_schema
\f1\fs24  on top, you get descriptions, defaults, and constraints too.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Field names in the schema must match the function parameters exactly.
\f1\b0  He had 
\f2\fs26 seats
\f1\fs24  in the function and 
\f2\fs26 seat_count
\f1\fs24  in the schema during the demo and flagged it as his own mistake \'97 there's no automatic mapping.\

\f0\b JSON schema is weaker than Pydantic.
\f1\b0  Declare 
\f2\fs26 location
\f1\fs24  as 
\f2\fs26 "type": "string"
\f1\fs24  in a raw JSON schema and pass an integer: no error. Python doesn't enforce type hints; Pydantic does. Prefer Pydantic.\
On the token cost of richer schemas: don't optimise for fewer tokens at the expense of correctness. Thirty extra tokens that get the right answer on the first call beat two calls.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 4. Reserved parameter names\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs26 \cf0 config
\f0\fs24  and 
\f3\fs26 runtime
\f0\fs24  cannot be used as tool argument names.
\f1\b0  LangChain reserves both.\
The failure mode is worth knowing: defining the tool succeeds silently. The error only appears when the tool is actually invoked \'97 a runtime error, not a definition-time one. The demo took a while to surface because the error was buried in the last message of the agent result.\
Why runtime rather than a syntax error: the same function might be decorated for a different framework that doesn't reserve those names, so LangChain doesn't block the definition.\
If you need a config parameter, rename it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 5. Binding vs execution\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs26 \cf0 model_with_tools = model.bind_tools([check_showtimes, book_seats])\
response = model_with_tools.invoke("Is Interstellar showing tonight? Book two seats for Rohan")\
\
for tool_call in response.tool_calls:\
    print(tool_call["name"], tool_call["args"])\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 The response has empty content and a populated 
\f2\fs26 tool_calls
\f1\fs24  list. Nothing executed.\
Flow: 
\f2\fs26 model + tools 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  bind_tools 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  model_with_tools 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  invoke 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  model decides it needs a tool 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  tool call populated, request only 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  create_agent is what actually runs it
\f1\fs24 .\
Repeated for the third session running, because people keep getting it wrong in interviews: 
\f0\b the model never calls a tool.
\f1\b0  ChatGPT and Claude are agentic applications, not models \'97 the confusion comes from there.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 6. Three sources of tools\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Your own functions
\f1\b0  \'97 the normal case\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 LangChain pre-built tools and toolkits
\f1\b0  \'97 Tavily search, and a long integrations list. These come with their own API keys and rate limits (Tavily gives 1,000 free calls). LangChain doesn't hand you free credentials.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Server-side / provider tools
\f1\b0  \'97 web search, code interpreter, built into the model itself\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 He opened the Tavily source on GitHub to make the point that pre-built tools aren't magic: a class with a name, a description, a well-defined 
\f2\fs26 args_schema
\f1\fs24 , an 
\f2\fs26 __init__
\f1\fs24  and a 
\f2\fs26 _run
\f1\fs24  method. Worth reading.\
On provider tools: when Claude or ChatGPT searches the web, that runs on their servers. It's a capability inside the model, not the model executing your tool, and it never touches your machine.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

