\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 8. 
\f3\fs39 return_direct=True
\f0\fs36 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Normal flow uses the LLM at least twice: once to decide which tool to call, once to summarise the tool output.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 user 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  agent 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  model ("call this tool with these args") 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  tool runs 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  result 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  model summarises 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  answer\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Sometimes you don't want that second pass.\
The example: a refund policy tool that returns verbatim policy text. Run it through the model and it "helpfully" reformats into bullets, adds "contact customer support", and generally introduces claims that were never in the policy. Even a system message saying 
\f5\i must be shown verbatim, never paraphrased
\f1\i0  didn't hold.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 @tool(return_direct=True)\
def get_refund_policy() -> str:\
    """Return the exact refund policy text."""\
    return POLICY_TEXT\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 With 
\f2\fs26 return_direct=True
\f1\fs24 , the tool's output goes straight back as the answer. Inspecting the messages shows the chain ending at the 
\f2\fs26 ToolMessage
\f1\fs24  \'97 no final AI turn. Fewer tokens as a side benefit.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 The Air Canada case
\f1\b0  was the cautionary tale: their chatbot told a bereaved passenger he could claim a discounted fare retroactively. The court held the airline liable. A policy written as "we go out of our way to be helpful to our customers\'85 but we do not offer refunds" is exactly the kind of text a model will soften.\
Caveat: nothing runs after a 
\f2\fs26 return_direct
\f1\fs24  tool. If you need further steps, or you want the model to sanity-check the output, this isn't the right tool for it. You can't have both verbatim output and model verification.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 9. Dynamic tool loading (problem stated, solution next class)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Three distinct problems, all solved with middleware in the next session:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Context bloat.
\f1\b0  Fifty tools at roughly 500 tokens each is 25k tokens before the user says anything. Beyond cost, the model gets confused choosing between them.\

\f0\b Authorisation.
\f1\b0  With 
\f2\fs26 standard_booking
\f1\fs24  and 
\f2\fs26 vip_lounge_booking
\f1\fs24  both bound, a normal user asking "book me a VIP lounge seat" got the VIP tool called. The model decided, and the model can be wrong. The tool should never have been loaded for that user in the first place. This is a guardrail problem, not a prompting problem.\

\f0\b Runtime discovery.
\f1\b0  If you point an agent at an MCP server mid-conversation, it starts with zero tools and has to load them during the run.\
Live evidence from Claude: a bare "hi" costs about 4k tokens, then climbs to 27\'9635k as connectors load. 
\f0\b Haiku loads all tools up front; Sonnet and Opus load them on demand
\f1\b0  \'97 one more reason to use a stronger model.\
Dynamic loading is not the default. It requires middleware (
\f2\fs26 wrap_model_call
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 wrap_tool_call
\f1\fs24 ), which is the next topic.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 10. Q&A worth keeping\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Agent vs bind_tools.
\f1\b0  Binding tells the model the tool exists. The agent can actually call it. The agent also invokes the model internally \'97 you pass the model in at creation and never call it yourself.\

\f0\b Multiple stores.
\f1\b0  Possible, but generally avoid it. Don't scatter state across storage solutions.\

\f0\b Store size limits.
\f1\b0  Not a LangChain question. How much data a store holds and how it scales is a database question.\

\f0\b Other frameworks.
\f1\b0  Google ADK, OpenAI Agents SDK, Claude Agent SDK, AutoGen, CrewAI, Pydantic AI. All do the same things: agents, tools, messages, memory. They can talk to each other via plain APIs or A2A (agent-to-agent), covered later.\

\f0\b Reserved names inside a schema.
\f1\b0  Whether 
\f2\fs26 config
\f1\fs24 /
\f2\fs26 runtime
\f1\fs24  are legal as fields inside an 
\f2\fs26 args_schema
\f1\fs24  rather than direct parameters \'97 untested, he said he'd check.\

\f0\b Guardrails.
\f1\b0  An umbrella term for anything that makes an agent more deterministic or less prone to hallucination. Everything in this session converges on it. Sophisticated tooling exists, but getting these fundamentals right from the start matters more.\

\f0\b Using AI to write this code faster.
\f1\b0  Claude Code writes at the level you can specify. Knowing that 
\f2\fs26 ToolStrategy
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 args_schema
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 return_direct
\f1\fs24 , and dynamic loading exist is what lets you ask for them. That first push is the difference between generated code and good generated code.\

\f0\b Evaluation and monitoring.
\f1\b0  LangSmith and other frameworks, covered later. For validating against internal data, use a dev/UAT database during development the way you would for any application.\

\f0\b Documentation.
\f1\b0  Repeated as the single skill worth having. Everything ChatGPT tells you is downstream of the docs anyway.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
